  <main id="main-content" class="guide-main" style="--accent:#06245B;--accent-soft:#F1F5F9;--accent-border:#CBD5E1;">
    <div class="container guide-layout">
      <article class="guide-card">
        <div class="guide-label">PipelineSync field notes</div>
        <h2>The practical breakdown</h2>
        <p class="intro">Duplicates are easy to dismiss as a database problem. In practice, they split activity history, confuse ownership, weaken segmentation, and force reps to spend time deciding which record to trust. The visible count is only the beginning of the cost.</p>
        <section><h3>Where the cost shows up</h3><p>A duplicate contact can receive conflicting outreach, miss a marketing suppression rule, or be routed to the wrong owner. A duplicate company can fragment deal history and make account-level reporting unreliable. Every disconnected record creates a small decision tax.</p><ul><li>Lost context when activity is split across records.</li><li>Incorrect routing when ownership is inconsistent.</li><li>Unreliable reporting when revenue is associated to different records.</li></ul></section><section><h3>Find patterns before fixing records</h3><p>Do not start by merging everything that looks similar. Segment suspected duplicates by source, object, owner, last activity, and match confidence. The goal is to understand why records were created and which fields should determine the primary record.</p><ul><li>Compare email, domain, phone, external ID, and normalized name.</li><li>Identify imports and integrations that create repeat records.</li><li>Protect consent, lifecycle, and ownership fields during merges.</li></ul></section><section><h3>Turn cleanup into prevention</h3><p>A cleanup project pays off when it changes the system that created the problem. Use stable integration keys, normalization rules, controlled imports, and a visible exception queue. Then measure new duplicate creation alongside the cleanup backlog.</p><ul><li>Upsert on a stable external ID where possible.</li><li>Normalize phone, country, domain, and key picklist values.</li><li>Review new suspected duplicates on a weekly cadence.</li></ul></section>
        <section>
          <h3>What to change this week</h3>
          <p>Use this short checklist to turn the article into an operating improvement.</p>
          <div class="guide-checklist"><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Duplicate definition agreed</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Match confidence rules set</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Primary-record hierarchy documented</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Consent fields protected</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Creation sources identified</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Prevention monitoring live</div></div></div>
        </section>
        <div class="guide-callout"><p><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> The real cost of duplicate data is the loss of confidence. Clean records matter because people make faster, better decisions when the CRM tells one consistent story.</p></div>
